WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:05.400
Continuing our time with conditions, I will&nbsp;
introduce a potential problem with our score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.680
and level code. I have the same code in this&nbsp;
file, 213-else, from the previous file. I will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:23.040
change the score to 3000 and run the code. What&nbsp;
displayed? Nothing. That might be the logic you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:28.240
want in an app like this, but you will likely&nbsp;
want a message to display if a player has yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.440
to reach a level so that the player at least&nbsp;
knows what is going on within a game and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:39.200
avoid overall confusion within the app. The else&nbsp;
keyword allows one to perform an action that runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.640
as part of a condition when the if condition is&nbsp;
not true and none of the other elif conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:52.920
are true. Listen to a real-life application of&nbsp;
else. If it is sunny and over 80 degrees outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:01:01.000
I will go to the beach. Else, if it’s sunny, I&nbsp;
will play golf. Else, I will stay at home. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.320
stay at home part applies if it’s not sunny at&nbsp;
all. For our code example, we want to let the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.240
game player know that if a target score has&nbsp;
not been exceeded, the player needs to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:19.320
to reach a level. Thus, starting on line 7, I&nbsp;
will add the else keyword with a colon. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:27.440
on the following line, I will indent four spaces&nbsp;
to match these indents and add this statement:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:38.120
print(“You need to score higher to reach a&nbsp;
level”). Then, I will skip a line and add a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:46.120
print statement that is not indented, thanking&nbsp;
them for playing. Recall that statements not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:52.280
indented are not part of a condition and, thus,&nbsp;
should always run. Before running this code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:58.680
know this about indents: Python recommends four&nbsp;
spaces for an indent, which many apps will give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:03.840
you when using the Tab key on the keyboard.&nbsp;
You can use the Tab key or add spaces manually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:10.240
but for the code to work correctly, you need&nbsp;
to be consistent in your indentation methods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:16.840
I always use four spaces, but the indentation&nbsp;
method is your choice. Just be consistent with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:26.160
your indentations. I will rerun this code now,&nbsp;
and we see the two messages we anticipated: that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:32.800
the player needs to get to a higher score, and the&nbsp;
player was thanked for playing. I will test this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:44.720
code again, using scores over 5000 and over 10000,&nbsp;
and, in each case, we see the results we want:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.040
that the player has reached a level matching the&nbsp;
score and that the player is thanked for playing.

